# Hash Chain System

{info:title=Cryptographic Integrity Engine}
The Hash Chain System provides the cryptographic foundation for tamper-evident storage in the PostgreSQL Blockchain Extension, using SHA-256 cryptographic hashing to create an unbreakable chain of data integrity.
{info}

{toc:printable=true|style=disc|maxLevel=3}

## Overview

### What is a Hash Chain?

{panel:title=Hash Chain Concept|borderColor=#2196F3|titleBGColor=#E3F2FD}
A hash chain is a cryptographic structure where each data element is linked to the previous one through cryptographic hash functions. In our blockchain table implementation:

* Each row contains a hash of its own data (`__curr_hash`)
* Each row references the hash of the previous row (`__prev_hash`)
* Any modification to historical data breaks the chain
* Chain verification detects tampering attempts
{panel}

### Visual Chain Structure

{code:title=Hash Chain Visualization}
Row 1: [Data: "user login"] 
       [Hash: SHA256(data + timestamp + counter)]
       [Prev: NULL]
         ↓
Row 2: [Data: "file access"]
       [Hash: SHA256(data + timestamp + counter + prev_hash)]
       [Prev: Hash_of_Row_1] ← Links to previous row
         ↓
Row 3: [Data: "logout"]
       [Hash: SHA256(data + timestamp + counter + prev_hash)]
       [Prev: Hash_of_Row_2] ← Links to previous row
         ↓
Row N: [Data: "..."]
       [Hash: SHA256(...)]
       [Prev: Hash_of_Row_(N-1)]
{code}

## Cryptographic Implementation

### SHA-256 Hash Function

{section}
{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=Why SHA-256?|borderColor=#4CAF50|titleBGColor=#E8F5E8}
**Security Properties:**
* 256-bit output (32 bytes)
* Cryptographically secure
* Collision-resistant
* Widely tested and standardized

**Performance:**
* Optimized implementations available
* Hardware acceleration support
* Reasonable computational cost
{panel}
{column}

{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=Hash Output Format|borderColor=#FF9800|titleBGColor=#FFF3E0}
**Binary Storage:**
* Stored as PostgreSQL `bytea` type
* 32 bytes per hash value
* 64 bytes per row (current + previous)

**Human Readable:**
* Hex encoding for display: `encode(__curr_hash, 'hex')`
* Base64 encoding option available
{panel}
{column}
{section}

### Hash Computation Algorithm

{expand:title=Complete Hash Computation Implementation}
{code:c|title=compute_curr_hash_with_counter Function}
bytea *
compute_curr_hash_with_counter(Relation rel, TupleTableSlot *slot,
                               TimestampTz ts, bytea *prev_hash, uint64 counter)
{
    SHA256_CTX context;
    unsigned char hash_output[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];
    
    // Initialize SHA-256 context
    SHA256_Init(&context);
    
    // 1. Add all user-defined column data
    for (int i = 0; i < slot->tts_tupleDescriptor->natts; i++)
    {
        if (!is_blockchain_system_column(TupleDescAttr(slot->tts_tupleDescriptor, i)->attname))
        {
            // Serialize column data in deterministic format
            Datum value = slot_getattr(slot, i + 1, &is_null);
            if (!is_null)
            {
                char *serialized = serialize_datum_deterministic(value, attr_type);
                SHA256_Update(&context, serialized, strlen(serialized));
                pfree(serialized);
            }
            else
            {
                // Include explicit NULL marker
                SHA256_Update(&context, "NULL", 4);
            }
        }
    }
    
    // 2. Add timestamp (temporal binding)
    char timestamp_str[64];
    snprintf(timestamp_str, sizeof(timestamp_str), "%ld", ts);
    SHA256_Update(&context, timestamp_str, strlen(timestamp_str));
    
    // 3. Add counter value (sequential binding)
    char counter_str[32];
    snprintf(counter_str, sizeof(counter_str), "%lu", counter);
    SHA256_Update(&context, counter_str, strlen(counter_str));
    
    // 4. Add previous hash (chain linking)
    if (prev_hash != NULL)
    {
        SHA256_Update(&context, VARDATA(prev_hash), VARSIZE(prev_hash) - VARHDRSZ);
    }
    else
    {
        // Genesis row - no previous hash
        SHA256_Update(&context, "GENESIS", 7);
    }
    
    // 5. Finalize hash computation
    SHA256_Final(hash_output, &context);
    
    // 6. Convert to PostgreSQL bytea
    bytea *result = (bytea *) palloc(VARHDRSZ + SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH);
    SET_VARSIZE(result, VARHDRSZ + SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH);
    memcpy(VARDATA(result), hash_output, SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH);
    
    return result;
}
{code}
{expand}

### Data Serialization

{expand:title=Deterministic Data Serialization}
{code:c|title=serialize_datum_deterministic Function}
static char *
serialize_datum_deterministic(Datum value, Oid typeid)
{
    switch (typeid)
    {
        case INT4OID:
            return psprintf("%d", DatumGetInt32(value));
            
        case INT8OID:
            return psprintf("%ld", DatumGetInt64(value));
            
        case TEXTOID:
        case VARCHAROID:
        {
            text *txt = DatumGetTextP(value);
            return text_to_cstring(txt);
        }
        
        case TIMESTAMPTZOID:
        {
            TimestampTz ts = DatumGetTimestampTz(value);
            return psprintf("%ld", ts);
        }
        
        case UUIDOID:
        {
            pg_uuid_t *uuid = DatumGetUUIDP(value);
            return psprintf("%02x%02x%02x%02x-%02x%02x-%02x%02x-%02x%02x-%02x%02x%02x%02x%02x%02x",
                           uuid->data[0], uuid->data[1], uuid->data[2], uuid->data[3],
                           uuid->data[4], uuid->data[5], uuid->data[6], uuid->data[7],
                           uuid->data[8], uuid->data[9], uuid->data[10], uuid->data[11],
                           uuid->data[12], uuid->data[13], uuid->data[14], uuid->data[15]);
        }
        
        // Add more types as needed
        default:
            ereport(ERROR,
                    (errcode(ERRCODE_FEATURE_NOT_SUPPORTED),
                     errmsg("unsupported data type for hash computation: %d", typeid)));
    }
}
{code}
{expand}

## Chain Management

### Previous Hash Retrieval

{panel:title=Previous Hash Lookup Strategy|borderColor=#673AB7|titleBGColor=#EDE7F6}
To maintain the chain, each new row must reference the hash of the most recently inserted row:

{code:c|title=get_previous_hash Implementation}
static bytea *
get_previous_hash(Relation rel)
{
    // Find the row with the highest counter value
    ScanKey scankey;
    SysScanDesc scan;
    HeapTuple tuple;
    bytea *prev_hash = NULL;
    uint64 max_counter = 0;
    
    // Scan to find the most recent row
    scan = systable_beginscan(rel, InvalidOid, false, NULL, 0, scankey);
    
    while ((tuple = systable_getnext(scan)) != NULL)
    {
        // Extract counter and hash from the tuple
        uint64 row_counter = extract_counter_from_tuple(tuple);
        if (row_counter > max_counter)
        {
            max_counter = row_counter;
            prev_hash = extract_current_hash_from_tuple(tuple);
        }
    }
    
    systable_endscan(scan);
    return prev_hash;
}
{code}
{panel}

### Genesis Row Handling

{expand:title=First Row (Genesis) Special Case}
The first row in any blockchain table has special handling:

{code:c|title=Genesis Row Logic}
static bytea *
handle_genesis_row(Relation rel, TupleTableSlot *slot, TimestampTz ts, uint64 counter)
{
    // For the first row, there is no previous hash
    bytea *genesis_hash = compute_curr_hash_with_counter(rel, slot, ts, NULL, counter);
    
    // Set previous hash to NULL in the slot
    slot_setattr(slot, get_prev_hash_attno(rel), (Datum) 0, true);
    
    return genesis_hash;
}
{code}

**Genesis Row Properties:**
* `__prev_hash` is NULL
* Hash computation includes "GENESIS" marker
* Counter value starts at 1
* Establishes the chain foundation
{expand}

## Chain Verification

### Integrity Verification Algorithm

{expand:title=Complete Chain Verification Implementation}
{code:c|title=verify_hash_chain SQL Function}
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION verify_hash_chain(table_name TEXT)
RETURNS TABLE(
    counter_value BIGINT,
    hash_valid BOOLEAN,
    error_message TEXT
) AS $$
DECLARE
    rec RECORD;
    computed_hash BYTEA;
    prev_hash_value BYTEA := NULL;
    row_count INTEGER := 0;
BEGIN
    -- Iterate through all rows in counter order
    FOR rec IN 
        EXECUTE format('SELECT *, __tx_lsn, __curr_hash, __prev_hash FROM %I ORDER BY __tx_lsn', table_name)
    LOOP
        row_count := row_count + 1;
        
        -- For first row, previous hash should be NULL
        IF row_count = 1 THEN
            IF rec.__prev_hash IS NOT NULL THEN
                RETURN QUERY SELECT rec.__tx_lsn, false, 'Genesis row should have NULL previous hash';
                CONTINUE;
            END IF;
        ELSE
            -- For subsequent rows, verify previous hash matches
            IF rec.__prev_hash IS DISTINCT FROM prev_hash_value THEN
                RETURN QUERY SELECT rec.__tx_lsn, false, 'Previous hash does not match expected value';
                CONTINUE;
            END IF;
        END IF;
        
        -- Recompute hash for this row (simplified - actual implementation in C)
        -- This would call the C function: compute_and_verify_hash(rec)
        computed_hash := compute_row_hash_c_function(table_name, rec.__tx_lsn);
        
        IF computed_hash = rec.__curr_hash THEN
            RETURN QUERY SELECT rec.__tx_lsn, true, NULL::TEXT;
        ELSE
            RETURN QUERY SELECT rec.__tx_lsn, false, 'Hash verification failed';
        END IF;
        
        -- Store current hash for next iteration
        prev_hash_value := rec.__curr_hash;
    END LOOP;
    
    IF row_count = 0 THEN
        RETURN QUERY SELECT 0::BIGINT, true, 'Empty table - chain valid';
    END IF;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;
{code}
{expand}

### Performance Optimized Verification

{section}
{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=Verification Strategies|borderColor=#4CAF50|titleBGColor=#E8F5E8}
**Full Chain Verification:**
* Verifies every row in sequence
* Computationally expensive O(n)
* Suitable for maintenance operations

**Spot Verification:**
* Verifies random sample of rows
* Much faster for large tables
* Good for regular monitoring
{panel}
{column}

{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=Verification Performance|borderColor=#FF9800|titleBGColor=#FFF3E0}
| Table Size | Full Verification | Spot Check (1%) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **1,000 rows** | ~10ms | ~1ms |
| **100,000 rows** | ~1s | ~10ms |
| **1,000,000 rows** | ~10s | ~100ms |
| **10,000,000 rows** | ~100s | ~1s |
{panel}
{column}
{section}

## Security Properties

### Tamper Detection

{panel:title=Tamper Detection Capabilities|borderColor=#F44336|titleBGColor=#FFEBEE}
The hash chain system detects various types of tampering:

**Data Modification:**
* Any change to user data breaks the hash
* Immediate detection during verification

**Historical Tampering:**
* Changes to old rows break the chain
* All subsequent rows show verification failures

**Row Insertion/Deletion:**
* Missing rows create hash chain gaps
* Counter sequence validation detects gaps

**Metadata Tampering:**
* System column modifications break hashes
* Timestamp or counter changes detectable
{panel}

### Attack Resistance

{expand:title=Cryptographic Security Analysis}
**Hash Function Security (SHA-256):**
* Collision resistance: 2^128 operations
* Preimage resistance: 2^256 operations
* Second preimage resistance: 2^256 operations

**Chain Security Properties:**
* **Forward integrity**: Old data cannot be changed without detection
* **Temporal binding**: Timestamps prevent replay attacks
* **Sequential binding**: Counter values prevent reordering
* **Cumulative security**: Each row reinforces the entire chain

**Practical Attack Scenarios:**
* **Database compromise**: Hashes detect unauthorized changes
* **Backup tampering**: Verification reveals modified data
* **Insider threats**: System logs capture tampering attempts
{expand}

## Performance Considerations

### Hash Computation Overhead

{section}
{column:width=50%}
| Component | Time per Row | Optimization |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Data Serialization** | ~1µs | Efficient type handling |
| **SHA-256 Computation** | ~1-2µs | Hardware acceleration |
| **Previous Hash Lookup** | ~1µs | Caching last hash |
| **Storage** | ~0.5µs | Optimized bytea handling |
{column}

{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=Total Hash Overhead|borderColor=#4CAF50|titleBGColor=#E8F5E8}
**~4µs per INSERT operation**

This represents approximately **4% overhead** for typical INSERT operations, providing cryptographic integrity with minimal performance impact.
{panel}
{column}
{section}

### Memory Usage

{expand:title=Hash System Memory Profile}
**Per-Operation Memory:**
* SHA-256 context: 256 bytes
* Hash output buffer: 32 bytes
* Temporary serialization: Variable (typically <1KB)

**Persistent Memory:**
* Previous hash cache: 32 bytes per table
* No global hash state maintained
* Memory usage scales O(1) with table count
{expand}

### Optimization Techniques

{tip:title=Hash Performance Optimizations}
**Hardware Acceleration:**
* Use CPU SHA instructions when available
* Intel SHA-NI instruction set support
* ARM SHA-256 acceleration

**Caching Strategies:**
* Cache previous hash to avoid lookups
* Batch hash computation for bulk inserts
* Optimize data serialization paths

**Memory Management:**
* Use stack allocation for small buffers
* Minimize memory copying operations
* Efficient bytea handling
{tip}

## Integration with Counter System

### Counter-Hash Coordination

{panel:title=Counter and Hash Integration|borderColor=#9C27B0|titleBGColor=#F3E5F5}
The hash chain system is tightly integrated with the global counter system:

**Sequential Binding:**
* Counter value included in hash computation
* Prevents row reordering attacks
* Ensures temporal ordering

**Atomic Operations:**
* Counter increment and hash computation are atomic
* No possibility of counter/hash mismatch
* Transaction consistency maintained

**Recovery Coordination:**
* Hash verification validates counter sequence
* Missing counters detected through chain gaps
* Counter recovery uses hash chain for validation
{panel}

### Cross-Table Chain Independence

{expand:title=Multi-Table Hash Chain Architecture}
Each blockchain table maintains its own independent hash chain:

{code:title=Independent Chain Architecture}
Table A: Row1 → Row2 → Row3 → ...
         (Independent chain)

Table B: Row1 → Row2 → Row4 → ...
         (Independent chain)

Global Counter: Provides unique sequencing across all tables
Hash Chains: Provide integrity within each table
{code}

**Benefits:**
* Table-level verification granularity
* Independent table lifecycle management
* Parallel verification possible
* Reduced verification scope for large systems
{expand}

## SQL Interface

### Hash Chain Functions

{expand:title=User-Accessible Hash Chain Functions}
{code:sql|title=Hash Chain SQL Functions}
-- Verify entire hash chain for a table
SELECT * FROM verify_hash_chain('audit_log');

-- Get hash chain information for specific rows
SELECT * FROM get_hash_chain_info('audit_log', 42);

-- Export hash chain for external verification
SELECT 
    __tx_lsn as counter,
    encode(__curr_hash, 'hex') as current_hash,
    encode(__prev_hash, 'hex') as previous_hash,
    __tx_timestamp as timestamp
FROM audit_log 
ORDER BY __tx_lsn;

-- Manual hash verification for specific row
SELECT verify_row_hash('audit_log', __row_id) FROM audit_log WHERE id = 123;
{code}
{expand}

